
extends ../framework/layout

block head

    link(rel='stylesheet', href='/stylesheets/docs/monitoring.css')

block body

    body.surface-level-1
        .content
            .title.surface-level-2 Server Monitoring
            p Review server health (sessions, memory), clear caches, inspect configuration and environment variables and manage storage.
            img(src='../images/docs/monitoring-1.png' style='display:block;max-width:960px;')
            h1 Access
            p This utility requires a valid PLM login, membership in the tenant's 
                strong Administration [SYSTEM]
                |  group (verified server-side on every request) and a monitoring secret. Without all three, the page and its APIs stay locked. Provide the monitoring secret by setting
            .code exports.monitoringSecret
            p in your environment file (environment.js, or the file in folder /environments used to start the server). 
                br
                |Like all values in these environment files, you can also use the following environment variable instead. This variable takes priority if both are set:
            .code MONITORING_SECRET
            h1 What it shows
            ul
                li Server Environment Variables
                li Server startup information
                li Active user sessions (count, user name, user mail address, PLM login state, per-session cache size) 
                li System memory consumption, sampled every 5 seconds and charted over time
                li User based cache memory consumption, sampled every 5 seconds and charted over time
                li A button to clear these shared and per-session data caches
                li Buttons to view the server's environment and settings files
                li Server uptime and button to perform a server restart
                li Storage statistics for the caching folder 
                    strong /storage
                    | including functionality for cleanup
                li Live feed of the server console output
            p The dashboard contents refresh automatically on defined intervals, but users can always enforce a refresh with the button in main toolbar
            h1 Advanced Capabilities 
            p Admins can use the dashboard to perform changes on the environment file, by overriding parameters in the settings.js file: 
            ul 
                li Click the edit button in the 
                    strong Edit Server Seetings 
                    |card to open the editor
                li Check the override option for the values to be changed and provide the right input in column value
                li Once done, use the given button to save your changes and restart the server.
            img(src='../images/docs/monitoring-2.png' style='display:block;max-width:960px;')
            //- h1 Updating the server from GitHub
            //- p The Deployment card compares the local checkout against the latest commit on
            //-     a(href='https://github.com/dickmans/plm-extensions' target='_blank') github.com/dickmans/plm-extensions
            //-     |  (branch main) and shows when the server was last updated through this page.
            //- p The
            //-     strong  Update &amp; Restart
            //-     |  button pulls the latest code (fast-forward only - it never overwrites diverged history) and restarts the server process. It refuses to run if the working tree has uncommitted changes, listing the files that need to be committed, stashed or discarded first.
            //- p While the update is applied, the server process restarts. During that short window every visitor - not only admins - sees a maintenance page explaining the server is being updated, which retries automatically and also offers a manual retry button. Because sessions are kept in memory only, everyone, including the administrator who triggered the update, needs to log in again once the server is back online.